WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:07.130
Right now, we're facing the biggest Ebola
outbreak we've ever seen in history.

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:11.690
More than 2,000 people have died and that's
more than the total number of people who have

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:15.730
ever died from Ebola since the first recorded
outbreak in 1976.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:22.000
And the bulk of the deaths have happened within
the last month, so things aren't getting better.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.269
There are a couple of things that have helped
this outbreak really spin out of control and

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:28.279
one is the fact that the response just wasn't
fast enough.

00:00:28.279 --> 00:00:32.430
It wasn't until August that the World Health
Organization declared this a public health

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.660
emergency, and that was 5 months after the
first international spread.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:41.910
In the past, Ebola outbreaks tended to be
in really rural, remote areas, and this time

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:46.930
it wound up in a border region in West Africa,
where people might cross the border for work,

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.850
or they might cross to go to the market.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.020
With that, you get international spread.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.670
This is a region of Africa that's never seen
Ebola, so they didn't have the awareness of

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.920
what this disease was in the population.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.140
These are also regions of Africa that have
some of the lowest literacy rates, so that

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.890
made mounting a public health information
campaign all the more difficult.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:07.890
And there was fear.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.820
There was mistrust of health professionals.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.750
People, in this part of the world, they're
used to being cared for by their families

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:17.990
when they're sick in the home, and in this
case they have to be ripped from their homes

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.290
and put into containment facilities.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.280
And these are places that have some of the
weakest and poorest health systems in the

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:25.280
world.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.970
In some of the hospitals there are days when
they just don't have gloves, gowns, and masks.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:33.640
Because of this, there have been health care
workers who have just walked off the job.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.180
They don't want to put themselves at risk
anymore, and I don't know if you can really

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.180
blame them.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.320
In Liberia people are taking their family
members to the hospitals, but being turned

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.320
away.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.520
There are no free beds for Ebola treatment
in the country right now.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:50.840
So as a result, Ebola's going back into the
community and spreading further.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.880
We could look at this epidemic as a kind of
warning that we need more robust global health

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.590
systems, especially in a world that's increasingly
connected.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:03.920
That's something that we don't have now, and
that's one of the reasons why we've seen things

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.980
get so out of control in this outbreak.

